design:
This task is the prerequisite for T2–T7. All composition blocks (T2–T6) and the accent reactivity system (T7) depend on the scaffold being in place.

The `PlaygroundBlockConfig` interface stub should at minimum include:
```ts
interface PlaygroundBlockConfig {
  id: string;
  baseSchema: string | null;   // null = no schema class (neutral defaults)
  accentElements: string[];    // CSS selectors within the block that get the accent schema
}
```

This will be expanded by T7 but stubbing it here ensures T2–T6 can start without coupling to T7.

description:
Implement the accent reactivity configuration system for the playground page. This task formalizes the ad-hoc schema bindings from T2–T6 into a typed configuration object, and adds a sidebar editor that lets the user experiment with different mappings at runtime.

## What this task delivers

1. A `PlaygroundBlockConfig` TypeScript type (stubbed in T1, completed here) with a full schema mapping shape
2. A `DEFAULT_BLOCK_CONFIGS` constant defining the base configuration for all 6 blocks
3. A sidebar editing UI that displays the current mapping for each block and allows toggling which elements respond to the accent picker
4. Reactive propagation: when the user edits the mapping in the sidebar, the affected blocks re-render without a page reload

## TypeScript shape

```ts
// apps/showcase/src/pages/playground-config.ts

export interface BlockElementConfig {
  /** CSS class selector fragment (without the dot) applied to the element */
  selector: string;
  /** Label shown in the sidebar editor */
  label: string;
  /** Whether this element currently responds to the accent schema from the picker */
  accentReactive: boolean;
}

export interface PlaygroundBlockConfig {
  id: string;
  /** Human-readable title for the sidebar */
  title: string;
  /** Schema applied to the block root wrapper. null = no schema class (neutral) */
  baseSchema: string | null;
  /** Elements within the block that CAN be accent-reactive */
  elements: BlockElementConfig[];
}

export const DEFAULT_BLOCK_CONFIGS: PlaygroundBlockConfig[] = [
  {
    id: 'login',
    title: 'Login / Sign-up',
    baseSchema: 'slate',
    elements: [
      { selector: 'btn-submit', label: 'Submit button', accentReactive: true },
      { selector: 'input-focus', label: 'Input focus ring', accentReactive: true },
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'product',
    title: 'Product Card',
    baseSchema: 'slate',
    elements: [
      { selector: 'price', label: 'Price badge', accentReactive: true },
      { selector: 'add-to-cart', label: 'Add to Cart button', accentReactive: true },
      { selector: 'chip.active', label: 'Active size chip', accentReactive: true },
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'player',
    title: 'Music Player',
    baseSchema: 'gray',
    elements: [
      { selector: 'progress-fill', label: 'Progress bar', accentReactive: true },
      { selector: 'ctrl-btn.play', label: 'Play button', accentReactive: true },
      { selector: 'playlist-item.active', label: 'Active playlist row', accentReactive: true },
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'dashboard',
    title: 'Dashboard',
    baseSchema: 'sand',
    elements: [
      { selector: 'stat-card.accent', label: 'Accent stat card', accentReactive: true },
      { selector: 'toggle.on', label: 'Toggle (on state)', accentReactive: true },
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'pricing',
    title: 'Pricing Table',
    baseSchema: null,
    elements: [
      { selector: 'tier-card.featured', label: 'Pro tier card', accentReactive: true },
      { selector: 'tier-card.enterprise', label: 'Enterprise tier (complement)', accentReactive: false },
      { selector: 'cta.solid', label: 'Pro CTA button', accentReactive: true },
    ]
  }
];
```

## Sidebar editor UI

The left sidebar (introduced in T1) renders the block config list. For each block:
- Block title as a section header
- `baseSchema` displayed as a read-only chip (e.g. 'base: slate')
- For each element: a toggle row showing the element label + an on/off toggle
  - When toggled OFF: that element loses its `line-schema-{accent}` class and falls back to the block's base schema
  - When toggled ON: the element gets `line-schema-{accent}` class applied

State is held in a `@state() private _configs: PlaygroundBlockConfig[]` array on `sc-page-playground`. The config is passed down to each block's render method.

## Reactivity flow

```
nav schema picker → sc-app._schema → sc-page-playground.schema (property)
  → sc-page-playground._configs (state, defines which elements are accent-reactive)
    → block render methods: classMap({ [`line-schema-${schema}`]: config.accentReactive })
```

No external state management needed — Lit's property/state system is sufficient.

notes:
ARCH UPDATE: Navigation is now panel-based (PanelKey), not URL-routed. Home is the default panel. UI font is Geist (sans), mono (IBM Plex Mono) for tokens/code only. Dark mode is default, use :host([light]) for light mode. Use semantic tokens (--line-high-contrast, --line-low-contrast, etc.) for all UI colors, NOT raw --line-gray-N stops.

NEW SECTIONS ADDED:
- Intro blurb: "the design system for the AI — future is bright!" between hero and stats. Style as a lead/subtitle with --line-low-contrast, larger than body text but smaller than h1.
- CSS usage snippet: Show a minimal educational example demonstrating both theming layers:
  ```css
  /* Color schema — pick your palette */
  body.line-schema-violet { }

  /* Mode: two layers work together */

  /* 1. Native browser property — drives CSS light-dark() resolution
        and inherits through shadow DOM boundaries */
  html { color-scheme: dark; }

  /* 2. Class on <html> — needed because shadow/elevation tokens
        use partial HSL values that cannot use light-dark() */
  html.dark { }

  /* Semantic tokens adapt to both layers automatically */
  .card {
    background: var(--line-subtle-background);
    color: var(--line-high-contrast);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-subtle-border);
    box-shadow: var(--line-shadow-3);
  }
  ```
  Use sc-code-block component for syntax highlighting. This snippet should be live — it reflects the current schema/mode chosen by the user in the nav. The goal is to educate: you need BOTH color-scheme (for light-dark()) AND .dark/.light classes (for shadow HSL tokens) working together.

description:
File: packages/theme/src/style.css

Approximately 50+ var(--size-*) and var(--font-*) references with inline fallbacks remain throughout the file. These follow the same unprefixed Open Props pattern that was cleaned up in line-ui-gn3 for the dead fallback block.

Examples of the pattern still present:

    var(--size-3, ...)
    var(--font-size-2, ...)
    var(--font-weight-7, ...)

These should be migrated to their --line-* prefixed equivalents (e.g., --line-size-3, --line-font-size-2) consistent with the naming convention established in packages/theme/src/tokens/ and the broader Phase 0 branding refactor described in PRD §9.14.

The inline fallbacks can be dropped once the corresponding --line-* tokens are confirmed present in the tokens barrel (tokens.css).

This is follow-up work to line-ui-gn3 and should be scoped as a dedicated migration pass over style.css.
